{"id": "7aea012c-1782-4c5c-b041-98fa61326561", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll look into this using the WAF log investigation workflow — querying the WAF logs in Athena for `18.206.238.200`'s activity against `response-id-on-page` over that window, checking what rules it's hitting, and pulling together enough context (patterns, intent, false-positive risk) to judge whether blocking is the right call.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_EaUiLIYzO944F1BKfOnAHb", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"deep_research": true, "prompt": "{
  "task": "Determine who/what client IP 18.206.238.200 is based on its traffic against AWS WAF, and assess whether blocking it on the CloudFront WebACL 'response-id-on-page' is the right security move.",
  "context": "Security team wants to block IP 18.206.238.200 on CloudFront WebACL 'response-id-on-page' (us-east-1), which protects website.a.example.com. The user wants to know: (1) who/what this IP appears to be (e.g. legitimate crawler/CDN/cloud provider, scanner, bot, attacker), and (2) whether blocking is justified given its observed behavior, or if it risks blocking legitimate traffic (false positive). Use the aws-waf-log-investigation skill for methodology — it covers querying WAF logs via Athena, WAF CloudWatch metrics, and WebACL rule configuration, and how to reach conclusions WAF evidence can support.",
  "resources": [
    "WebACL: response-id-on-page (us-east-1, protects website.a.example.com)",
    "Athena table: waf_analysis_tmp.waf_logs_response_id_on_page",
    "Athena workgroup: devops-agent",
    "Target IP: 18.206.238.200"
  ],
  "timeframe": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z/2026-10-15T00:00:00Z",
  "criteria": "Pull all WAF log entries for this specific IP across the full window: request volume over time, URIs/paths targeted, user agents, HTTP methods, matched rules/rule groups and actions (ALLOW/BLOCK/COUNT/CAPTCHA/CHALLENGE), country/ASN if available, and whether behavior looks like a legitimate bot/crawler/monitoring service vs malicious scanning, credential stuffing, SQLi/XSS/path traversal attempts, or DDoS/flood activity. Check current WebACL rule configuration to see if any rule is already acting on this IP (e.g. running in Count mode) and whether switching to Block would be a config change vs a new rule. Reach a clear recommendation grounded only in what the WAF evidence supports."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Analyze WAF logs in Athena for IP 18.206.238.200 against WebACL response-id-on-page"}], "created_at": null}
